{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. First, we extract all outcomes from both lotteries without considering their probabilities: A has outcomes [6000, 0], B has outcomes [4000, 2000, 0]. Combining all unique outcomes: [6000, 0, 4000, 2000]. The average outcome is the unweighted mean: (6000 + 0 + 4000 + 2000 + 0) / 5 = 12000 / 5 = 2400 Euros. (Note: although 0 appears twice, we consider unique outcomes only once per the instruction of taking the mean of outcomes, not weighted by probability or frequency.) Outcomes higher than 2400: 6000 and 4000. Now, count how many outcomes in each lottery are above 2400: Lottery A has one outcome (6000 > 2400); Lottery B has one outcome (4000 > 2400). Both have one outcome above the average. Thus, the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
